-- statement:
--   Exact primorial certificate for the primes up to $3989$:
--   $$\prod_{p \le 3989,\; p \text{ prime}} p = N \quad\text{and}\quad \prod_{p \le 3989,\; p \text{ prime}} (p-1) = D,$$
--   with $N$ and $D$ the two explicit integers stated in the formal statement (a 1699-digit primorial and its shifted product). This is a pure finite data certificate: it anchors the Rosser–Schoenfeld product-bound legs above $4000$ the same way the primorial certificates at $691$, $1049$, $1499$, $1999$, $2477$, $2999$ and $3499$ anchor the legs below, so that each range leg only has to telescope the Euler product over its own primes instead of re-verifying the primorial from scratch.
-- source:
--   Pure finite computation certificate (companion to TaoFivePrimes.primorial_certificate_691, TaoFivePrimes.primorial_certificate_1049, TaoFivePrimes.primorial_certificate_1499, TaoFivePrimes.primorial_certificate_1999, TaoFivePrimes.primorial_certificate_2477, TaoFivePrimes.primorial_certificate_2999 and TaoFivePrimes.primorial_certificate_3499): the exact values of the primorial $\prod_{p\le 3989} p$ and of $\prod_{p\le 3989}(p-1)$, to serve as the verified anchor for the range legs of Rosser–Schoenfeld Theorem 23 (4.10) above $4000$.

import Mathlib.NumberTheory.PrimeCounting

namespace TaoFivePrimes
theorem primorial_certificate_3989 :
    ∏ p ∈ Nat.primesLE 3989, (p : ℕ) =  ∧
    ∏ p ∈ Nat.primesLE 3989, ((p : ℕ) - 1) =  := by sorry
end TaoFivePrimes
